  },
  {
   "role": "Division / corps field hospital",
   "notes": "Further back; major surgery. Staffed by one surgeon in charge plus two assistants (supplies, records); the three best operators, not the highest-ranking, did the cutting",
   "tier": "documented",
   "src": "NMCWM-Letterman"
  },
  {
   "role": "General hospital",
   "notes": "Cities; later Camp Letterman",
   "tier": "documented",
   "src": "NMCWM-Letterman"
  },
  {
   "role": "Ambulance Corps",
   "notes": "Created Aug 1862 under medical control. Command ladder: captain per corps, 1st lieutenant per division, 2nd lieutenant per brigade, sergeant per regiment. Each ambulance: a driver and two stretcher-bearers (three privates)",
   "tier": "documented",
   "src": "Wiki-Amb"
  },
  {
   "role": "Ambulances per unit",
   "notes": "Per infantry regiment two two-horse ambulances, one four-horse ambulance, one medical wagon (Wikipedia); another source: one cart and two ambulances per regiment. Unsettled; four-wheel spring ambulances had largely replaced two-wheel by 1862-63; each carries two stretchers",
   "tier": "documented, with conflict",
   "src": "Wiki-Amb; HN-Letterman"
  },
  {
   "role": "Army of the Potomac ambulances",
   "notes": "About 928 in the permanent corps (NPS essay)",
   "tier": "documented",
   "src": "NPS-Essay5"
  },
  {
   "role": "Gettysburg reality",
   "notes": "Hooker cut medical transport in June 1863 to about two wagons per brigade; on Jul 1-2 supply trains were sent about 25 miles to the rear; medicine wagons came with ambulances, tents did not reach the field until Jul 5; Brinton's supplies arrived the evening of Jul 4. Each corps left six ambulances and four wagons to carry wounded to the rail depot; the Cavalry Corps left four",
   "tier": "documented",
   "src": "Letterman-Exc"
  },
  {
   "role": "Ambulance Corps losses",
   "notes": "1 officer and 4 privates killed, 17 wounded; some wounded fetched after daylight from beyond the farthest pickets under fire. By early morning Jul 4 no wounded remained on the field within Union lines",
   "tier": "documented",
   "src": "Letterman-Exc"
  },
  {
   "role": "Confederate side",
   "notes": "Lafayette Guild, Lee's medical director. Ambulance trains left Jul 4 by two routes (Cashtown and Fairfield) with \"broken-down\" cavalry escort; the Fairfield column was hit by Union cavalry; wounded were concentrated at Williamsport and crossed the Potomac by raft and ferry. Guild says every available means of transport was used and describes no ambulance shortage",
   "tier": "documented",
   "src": "Guild-OR; Wiki-Guild"
  },
  {
   "role": "Confederate evacuation animation",
   "notes": "The \"shortage of ambulances\" claim is widely repeated but is not in Guild's report. Animate improvised wagons (requisitioned local and captured vehicles), flagged unverified",
   "tier": "inferred",
   "src": "notes inference"
  },
  {
   "role": "Stretcher-bearer methods, bandsmen, blankets as litters, wheelbarrows, hospital stewards, 1863 training",
   "notes": "No source retrieved",
   "tier": "open gap",
   "src": "n/a"
  }
